-- 389: the lessons stop teaching the warning.
--
-- Evidence map C1: Hiniker, Suh, Cao and Kientz, CHI 2016 (children aged 1
-- to 5) found warned endings were followed by more upset, and endings went
-- better at a natural stopping point or as part of a routine. PR 1452 took
-- the warning out of the scripts, the app and the printables. Two lessons
-- were built on it, and Justin said rewrite both (7 November 2026).
--
-- THE EDITS
--   Foundation, "When the screen goes to sleep": the five minute whisper
--     becomes the stopping place, picked before the screen goes on ("one
--     episode, then snack"). The wave and the next thing stay. Its two quiz
--     questions about the whisper now ask why we pick the stopping place
--     first and why the end of an episode is easier. the_idea, try_this and
--     key_message follow.
--   Foundation, "Big feelings and the screen": the opening recall line.
--   KS2, ks2-04 "Screen routines": the cool down lap is agree, finish, swap.
--     Agree the finishing line before you start, finish at it, swap to the
--     next thing. Slides, parent note and teacher notes, from content/modules.
--   KS2, ks2-05 "Gaming time and spend": the retrieval slide that recalls
--     the lap.
--
-- GUARDED. Each school deck must hash to its files as they were before this
-- change (both read equal from production on 7 November 2026) or already to
-- the new files; anything else is a hand edit and aborts. The Foundation
-- lesson must have exactly its old slides (md5) and old column text, and the
-- slides it carries over unchanged are compared to the live ones first. The
-- proof at the end recomputes every hash and compares every value.
-- Idempotent: a second run finds the new values, writes nothing and passes.
-- Backed up first. Embeddings are left alone, as in 371 and 375: no edit
-- changes what a lesson is about.
--
-- Generated by scripts/gen-389.mjs from content/modules and
-- scripts/gen-389.foundation.json, never by hand.

begin;

create table if not exists public.lessons_backup_389 as
  select * from public.lessons where id in ('3cc159b1-f3cd-46b9-9eb1-fc29c7899e40', '158dee38-97ba-4b45-ae2b-55711a3cbf4e');
alter table public.lessons_backup_389 enable row level security;

create table if not exists schools.school_lessons_backup_389 as
  select * from schools.school_lessons where module_id in ('ks2-04-screen-routines', 'ks2-05-gaming-time-spend');
alter table schools.school_lessons_backup_389 enable row level security;

-- ── Foundation: When the screen goes to sleep ────────────────────────────
do $$
declare
  cur public.lessons;
  nxt jsonb := $gc389$[{"body":"A sofa lesson about endings. We practise saying goodbye to the screen so the goodbye stops being scary.","type":"title","phase":"starter","title":"When the screen goes to sleep","script":"Recall first: what is our stop and tell rule? Stop, and tell a grown up. Today is a different kind of goodbye: how we say bye bye to the screen when it is time.","eyebrow":"Foundation · Ages 4 to 7 · Healthy balance","minutes":1,"character":"football"},{"why":"Endings feel big when they are a surprise. Our plan makes the ending small: a stopping place, a wave, a next thing.","type":"objective","gains":["I know our stopping place, like the end of the episode","I can wave goodbye to the screen","I know the next thing comes straight after"],"phase":"starter","minutes":1,"outcome":"I can say goodbye to the screen calmly when we reach our stopping place."},{"type":"keywords","phase":"starter","words":[{"word":"the stopping place","meaning":"Where the screen stops, picked before it goes on, like the end of the episode."},{"word":"the next thing","meaning":"What we run to after the screen: a snack, a story, a job."}],"script":"Say each word and let your child say it back. The stopping place and the next thing are the two halves of every calm ending.","heading":"Our special words","minutes":1},{"body":"Big feelings come when the screen stops in the middle of something. So we pick the stopping place before the screen goes on: one episode, then snack. When the episode ends, the screen is done.","type":"concept","emoji":"🏁","phase":"teach","script":"Say it the same way every time, before you press play: one episode, then it is snack. Then the show ends it, not you, and there is no countdown to argue with. When the end comes, tell them what comes next, not how long is left.","heading":"The stopping place","minutes":2},{"body":"When time is up, we wave and say bye bye screen, see you tomorrow. Then we go straight to the next thing: a snack, a story, a job helping your grown up. Endings are easier when something nice is waiting.","type":"concept","emoji":"👋","phase":"teach","script":"The wave sounds silly and works like magic: it gives the ending a shape. Have the next thing genuinely ready before the wave happens. A child running toward something is not fighting what they left.","heading":"Wave goodbye, then run to the next thing","minutes":2},{"type":"choice","phase":"practise","minutes":2,"options":[{"text":"Wave bye bye to the screen and go to the next thing","correct":true,"feedback":"Yes. The wave finishes it, and the next thing is waiting for you."},{"text":"Hold the screen tight and look away from your grown up","correct":false,"feedback":"Holding tight makes the goodbye bigger and slower. The wave makes it small and quick."}],"question":"The episode has finished, and that was our stopping place. What do we do?"},{"type":"choice","phase":"prove","minutes":2,"options":[{"text":"So I know where the end is before I start","correct":true,"feedback":"Exactly. You knew the end before the screen went on, so it is not a surprise."},{"text":"So I can ask for one more when we get there","correct":false,"feedback":"The stopping place is the end, not a place to ask for more. When we get there, the next thing starts."}],"question":"Why do we pick the stopping place before the screen goes on?"},{"type":"choice","phase":"prove","minutes":2,"options":[{"text":"Because episodes are always short","correct":false,"feedback":"Some are short, but that is not the magic. The magic is that the show has finished, so nothing gets cut off."},{"text":"Because the show has finished, so nothing is cut off","correct":true,"feedback":"That is it. Big feelings come from being stopped in the middle. At the end of the episode, the show is already done."},{"text":"They do not, endings feel horrible anyway","correct":false,"feedback":"Endings feel much easier when the show has already finished. Nothing gets cut off at our stopping place."}],"question":"Why do endings feel easier at the end of an episode?"},{"type":"choice","phase":"prove","minutes":2,"options":[{"text":"Grabbing the tablet back for one more go, so the cross feeling stops","correct":false,"feedback":"One more go makes the ending come round again, and the cross feeling with it. Wave bye bye and run to the next thing."},{"text":"Waving bye bye and running to the next thing","correct":true,"feedback":"The wave finishes it, the next thing catches you. Cross feelings shrink fast when your hands are busy."},{"text":"Shouting until the feeling goes away by itself","correct":false,"feedback":"Shouting feeds the cross feeling. A snack, a story or a job helping shrinks it much faster."}],"question":"The screen is done and your body still feels cross. What helps most?"},{"type":"recap","phase":"close","points":["We pick the stopping place before we start.","Wave bye bye screen, see you tomorrow.","Run straight to the next thing together."],"heading":"The lesson in three ticks","minutes":1},{"body":"Before bed, let your child choose what comes straight after screen time tomorrow, and have it ready. A snack, a story, a job with you. Their choice, waiting for them.","type":"tryit","phase":"close","script":"A next thing they chose themselves pulls twice as hard as one you announce at switch off time.","heading":"Pick tomorrow's next thing tonight","minutes":1},{"type":"digi","lines":["A stopping place, a wave, a next thing.","Endings are easier when nothing is a surprise.","Bye bye screen, see you tomorrow."],"phase":"close","heading":"DiGi says","minutes":1}]$gc389$::jsonb;
begin
  select * into cur from public.lessons where id = '3cc159b1-f3cd-46b9-9eb1-fc29c7899e40';
  if not found then
    raise exception '389: lesson 3cc159b1-f3cd-46b9-9eb1-fc29c7899e40 (When the screen goes to sleep) is missing';
