  if got is distinct from 'e02af09590b2065186339af3dfcf2942' then
    raise exception '389: ks2-04-screen-routines does not match its files (hash %), refusing to overwrite a hand edit', got;
  end if;
  update schools.school_lessons
     set slides = $gc389$[{"body":"One hour, one mission: build a screen routine that is yours, and earn the Routine Builder badge tonight.","type":"title","phase":"starter","title":"Screen routines that work","script":"Settle everyone with this slide up. Big claim to open: today you will build something most adults have never managed to build. Not a rule someone gives you. A routine YOU design, that actually works. The badge at the end of this mission is called Routine Builder, and you earn it tonight, at home, not in this room.","eyebrow":"KS2 · Years 3 to 6 · Module 4","minutes":1,"character":"bloop"},{"src":"https://schools.guidedchildhood.com/clips/hf_20260911_081136_e558411d-5dd9-413f-8c46-62eec87f1221.mp4","type":"video","phase":"starter","script":"Play the clip once through. Then ask: who decides when screen time ends in your house?","caption":"Being the boss of your screen","minutes":1,"alternative":{"spoken":["Your screen never says right, that is enough. So who does? You do. Today you learn how."],"described":"Bloop, the round green Planet Friend, stands at the front of a classroom and talks to the class like a coach, gesturing with both rounded arms. Bloop starts serious, then breaks into a wide encouraging smile."}},{"why":"Screens are brilliant and they are built to be hard to put down, even for grown ups. Willpower loses that fight almost every time, but a routine wins it, and a routine you design yourself is one you will actually keep.","type":"objective","gains":["Explain why a routine beats willpower every single time","Run the cool down lap: agree, finish, swap","Spot when a screen is quietly stealing sleep, dinner or homework time","Design one routine tonight that is yours, not one handed to you"],"phase":"starter","script":"Read the mission out loud, then the why. Then ask: hands up if you have ever said one more video and it turned into loads more. Put your own hand up too, honestly. Keep a rough count of hands. Tell them: that is not a you problem, and by the end of the hour you will know exactly why it happens and exactly how to beat it.","minutes":2,"outcome":"I can build one screen routine that works and stick to it."},{"mode":"pairs","type":"discussion","phase":"starter","prompt":"Tell your partner one thing you always do before you go to sleep. Anything at all, screens or not.","script":"Sixty seconds with the person next to you. One thing you always do before bed. Not what you should do, what you actually do. I will take three of them.","lookFor":"Everybody already has a routine, even the ones who have never called it that. That is the hook for the whole hour.","minutes":2,"seconds":60},{"type":"keywords","phase":"starter","words":[{"word":"routine","meaning":"A plan you decide once and then follow, so you do not have to fight the same battle every day."},{"word":"willpower","meaning":"Using effort in the moment to make yourself stop. It runs out, especially when you are tired."},{"word":"wind down","meaning":"The calm time before bed when your brain slows down and gets ready for sleep."},{"word":"sleep displacement","meaning":"When screen minutes push sleep minutes out of the way. The lost sleep never comes back."}],"script":"Say each word, class repeats it back. Spend longest on sleep displacement, it sounds fancy but it is simple: imagine your sleep is water in a cup, and every scrolling minute at bedtime pours a bit out. You cannot pour it back in the morning. That picture will come back later in the lesson.","heading":"Squad words for today","minutes":2},{"type":"choice","phase":"starter","script":"Retrieval from the last module. Thirty seconds of thinking time before any hands, then cold call two pupils and ask for the reasoning, not just the answer. If the room is shaky on this, spend one extra minute: real, pretend, or computer made, then ask before you believe, is the whole of last lesson in one line.","minutes":3,"options":[{"text":"Real, pretend, or computer made?","correct":true,"feedback":"That is the detective question from last time. A WOW picture is exactly the one to ask it about, because a computer can make a picture that looks just like a photo."},{"text":"If it looks real, it is real","correct":false,"feedback":"That was the trap from last lesson. Pictures can be made or changed, so our eyes alone cannot settle it."},{"text":"Only grown ups can tell if a picture is real","correct":false,"feedback":"Grown ups cannot tell by looking either. The skill is asking the question, and you already have it."}],"question":"Mission memory from last time. A game advert shows a prize that looks absolutely amazing. What did we learn to ask first?"},{"body":"Here is the honest truth: apps and games are built with no finishing line. Autoplay starts the next video before you decide anything, and there is always one more round. Willpower means fighting that pull in the moment, and even adults lose that fight most nights. A routine is different. You decide ONCE, when you are calm, and then the plan does the work. And here is the secret ingredient: a routine somebody hands you feels like a punishment, but a routine you design yourself is yours, and people stick to what is theirs.","type":"concept","emoji":"🛠️","phase":"teach","script":"This is the big idea of the whole hour, so land it slowly. Ask: who has ever been told to just get off the tablet, and it turned into an argument? Most hands go up. That is willpower against design, and design wins. Then the turn: so we are not going to try harder, we are going to design better. You are the designer. Do not rush past the last line, the child designing their own routine is what makes this module work.","heading":"Routines beat willpower","minutes":2},{"type":"diagram","phase":"teach","steps":[{"text":"Agree your finishing line before you start: one episode, this match, until the timer goes. Decided while you are calm, so the end is never a surprise.","emoji":"🤝","title":"Agree"},{"text":"Stop at a real finishing line: the end of the video, the round, the level. Never mid game, that is what makes stopping hurt.","emoji":"🏁","title":"Finish"},{"text":"Have the next thing ready and waiting: food, football, a book, the shower. An empty gap pulls you straight back to the screen.","emoji":"🔄","title":"Swap"}],"script":"Walk the three steps as they build and get the class chanting them back: agree, finish, swap. Explain the racing picture, a driver at 200 miles an hour does not slam the brakes, they do a cool down lap. Your brain at full game speed is the same. Then point at the three verdict chips: today you become routine detectives, and every situation gets one of these three verdicts. Routine ready means the lap is there. Needs a lap means part of the plan is missing. Willpower trap means no plan at all, just hoping to resist.","caption":"Three steps, every time screens need to end. Racing drivers do a cool down lap, they never just slam the brakes.","heading":"The cool down lap","minutes":3,"verdicts":["Routine ready","Needs a lap","Willpower trap"]},{"type":"choice","phase":"teach","script":"First check on the tool. Ten seconds of thinking time, hands down, then everyone holds up one, two or three fingers for the keycap number of their answer. Watch for pupils choosing the turn it off instantly option because it sounds strict and therefore right, that is a really useful wrong answer to unpack: this module is not about stopping harder, it is about stopping smarter. The lap is kind to you on purpose, which is why it works.","minutes":2,"options":[{"text":"Finish the level, then swap to the thing you lined up","correct":true,"feedback":"Exactly. You agreed the finishing line before you started, so the end of the level is the stop, and the swap is ready and waiting. That is the whole lap."},{"text":"Turn it off instantly, mid level, no matter what","correct":false,"feedback":"Slamming the brakes mid level is what makes stopping feel horrible, and horrible stops turn into arguments. Finish at a real finishing line, that is the point of the lap."},{"text":"Start another level, you will stop when you feel like it","correct":false,"feedback":"You will not feel like it, and that is not a you problem, it is the design of the game. Feeling like stopping is willpower, and willpower loses. The lap wins."}],"question":"You agreed to stop at the end of this level, then tea. The level is nearly done. What does the cool down lap say to do?"},{"mode":"pairs","type":"discussion","phase":"teach","prompt":"What is the hardest moment of YOUR day to put a screen down, and what makes it so hard?","script":"Sixty seconds, partner talk, start the timer on screen. Share your own hardest moment first, adults have one too and admitting it gives the room permission to be honest. After the chime take three answers and sort them out loud: after school, bedtime, mealtimes, homework. Tell them: those four moments are exactly where we are taking the lap next.","lookFor":"Honesty, and pupils spotting the no finishing line moments: autoplay at bedtime, one more round after school, the group chat that never ends. The best answers notice the design, not just the feeling.","minutes":2,"seconds":60,"extension":true},{"body":"Sleep is when your brain saves everything you learned today and gets you ready for tomorrow. Screens at bedtime attack sleep twice. First, bright screens and exciting clips tell your brain it is daytime, so you feel wide awake at the exact moment you should be winding down. Second, sleep displacement: every minute of scrolling is a minute taken straight out of your sleep, and it never comes back. That is why the strongest bedtime routine in the world is boring and brilliant: screens sleep outside the bedroom.","type":"concept","emoji":"😴","phase":"teach","script":"Connect back to the cup of water from the keywords: bedtime scrolling pours your sleep out, and mornings cannot refill it. Then be honest about why outside the bedroom beats on silent: if the phone is within reach, checking it costs nothing and willpower has to win every single time you stir in the night. If it is charging in the kitchen, the routine wins while you sleep. Ask: where do screens sleep in your house? No judgement, just gather answers, you will use them in the parent note conversation.","heading":"Where screens sleep matters","minutes":2},{"meta":"9:47pm · autoplay is on","text":"You said one more video 47 videos ago 😂😂 tag someone who is DEFINITELY still scrolling right now instead of sleeping","type":"scenario","image":"📱","label":"Mission file one: the bedtime spiral","phase":"teach","stats":"❤ 312K   ↻ 96K   💬 41K","avatar":"🌙","handle":"midnight.clips","prompt":"It is 9:47pm and the next video is already loading. Which step of the cool down lap is missing, and where did this spiral actually start?","script":"Read the post with a grin, it is funny because it is true, even the app is laughing at us. Then get serious with the two questions. Which step is missing: the finish, autoplay deletes the finishing line so the video never actually ends. Where did the spiral start: not at 9:47, it started when the phone came into the bedroom. The best routine move happened an hour before this screenshot. Land that hard: routines win battles before they start.","minutes":3,"platform":"feed","extension":true},{"type":"diagram","phase":"teach","steps":[{"text":"Autoplay starts the next video before you choose anything. There is no finishing line to stop at.","emoji":"🌀","title":"One more becomes ten more"},{"text":"Bright light and exciting clips tell your brain it is daytime. Wind down goes into reverse.","emoji":"🧠","title":"Your brain wakes back up"},{"text":"Every scrolling minute is a sleep minute poured away. It does not come back tomorrow.","emoji":"⏳","title":"Sleep gets displaced"},{"text":"Grumpy, foggy, worse at football, worse with friends. Yesterday's scroll sends today the bill.","emoji":"🥱","title":"The next morning pays the bill"}],"script":"Walk the spiral as it builds, then ask the class where the weakest link is. Answer: step one, and even better, before step one. Ask what breaks the spiral completely, and steer them to it: if the phone sleeps in the kitchen, the spiral cannot even start. Then the identity line: most people fight this spiral every night and lose. You are designing your way out of the fight entirely.","caption":"The lap breaks this spiral at step one. The phone charging outside the bedroom breaks it before it begins.","heading":"How the bedtime spiral steals your sleep","minutes":3},{"type":"choice","phase":"teach","script":"Ten seconds of thinking time, then everyone holds up one, two or three fingers for the keycap number. The dangerous option matters: some pupils will have heard scare stories about phones near heads. Be straight with them: we do not need scary made up reasons, the true reason is strong enough. Sleep displacement is real, measurable in how you feel tomorrow, and the fix costs nothing.","minutes":2,"options":[{"text":"Not really. Checking is still one reach away, so willpower has to win every single time you stir","correct":true,"feedback":"Exactly. Silent fixes the noise, not the pull. A phone within reach asks your willpower to win all night long. A phone in the kitchen asks it to win once, at 8pm."},{"text":"Yes, silent means it cannot wake you, so the bedroom is fine","correct":false,"feedback":"Silent fixes the pings but not the pull. The reach for one quick check is the spiral's front door, and it is still wide open under your pillow."},{"text":"No, because phones near your head are dangerous","correct":false,"feedback":"That is not the honest reason. The phone is not hurting you by being near you. The real cost is the checking and the scrolling, which pour your sleep away minute by minute."}],"question":"Your phone is on silent under your pillow. Problem solved?"},{"body":"Two more places where routines quietly win. Mealtimes: a screen at the table splits your attention in half, and the half that talks to your family is the half that matters, meals are where a family actually finds out about each other's day.","type":"concept","emoji":"🍝","phase":"teach","script":"Keep this warm and completely unpreachy, Bloop's rule is that we never make anyone feel bad about loving screens, Bloop loves them too. Ask: who has ever watched something at the table and realised they cannot remember eating? Laugh with them.","heading":"The table","minutes":1},{"body":"Homework: the screen next to you does not even need to be on to distract you, part of your brain keeps guarding it, waiting for it to light up. In both places the fix is the same and it is not a ban: it is a routine that gives screens their own time, so the table and the desk get yours.","type":"concept","phase":"teach","script":"Then the desk point, which surprises people: a phone face down next to your homework still costs you focus, because your brain spends effort ignoring it. Not a guilt trip, a design fact, and designers fix design problems.","heading":"And the desk","minutes":1},{"meta":"Group chat · 18 new messages","text":"GET ON NOW we are all in the lobby, round starts in 2 mins, homework can wait lol it is literally due tomorrow not tonight 😆","type":"scenario","image":"📚","label":"Mission file two: the homework battle","phase":"teach","avatar":"🎮","handle":"Squad Chat 🎮","prompt":"Homework is sitting there, the squad is waiting, the round starts in two minutes. What would a designed routine have already decided?","script":"Read it like a real message, pace and all, then ask the prompt. The point to land: in this moment, willpower has already lost, the pull is too strong at two minutes to kickoff. But a designed routine decided this hours ago: homework first with the phone in another room, the finishing line agreed, and the squad session is the SWAP, the reward waiting at the finish line. Ask the class to say what that pupil could message back. Best answer sounds like: on at 5, save me a spot. Not a no. A when.","minutes":2,"platform":"message"},{"mode":"groups","type":"discussion","phase":"teach","prompt":"Design mission: build a homework routine using agree, finish, swap. Decide exactly where each step goes.","script":"Groups of three or four, ninety seconds on the timer, this is the design challenge of the day. Every group must place all three steps somewhere. Collect one routine per group after the chime and test it out loud against the squad chat scenario: would this routine have survived GET ON NOW? Celebrate the group that put the gaming session after homework as the swap, that is the pro move, the screen becomes the reward instead of the enemy.","lookFor":"The finishing line agreed before screens start, not during: end of round or end of video, never mid anything. A real swap into homework, and the fun thing placed after homework as the reward. Bonus brilliance: the phone in a different room while working.","minutes":3,"seconds":90,"extension":true},{"type":"choice","phase":"teach","script":"Last check before practice. Ten seconds of thinking time, then everyone holds up one, two or three fingers for the keycap number. The phrase to send them into the worksheet with: routines win battles before they begin. Get the class to repeat it once. If anyone picked the promise to be sensible, do not scold it, name it as the most human answer on the slide, we have all made that promise, and that is exactly why the lap exists.","minutes":2,"options":[{"text":"Before the battle starts: agree the finishing line and the swap while you are calm","correct":true,"feedback":"That is the designer's move. Routines win battles before they begin. Deciding at 4pm is easy. Deciding mid round at 9pm is nearly impossible, for anyone."},{"text":"In the middle of the round, when you notice the time","correct":false,"feedback":"By mid round the pull is at full strength and you are asking willpower to fight it alone. The lap works because it is decided early, when deciding is easy."},{"text":"You do not need the lap if you promise yourself you will be sensible","correct":false,"feedback":"A promise to be sensible is willpower wearing a disguise. The apps are designed by teams of adults to beat that promise. Beat their design with your design."}],"question":"When is the smartest time to decide your cool down lap on a school night?","extension":true},{"type":"interactive","phase":"practise","config":{"prompt":"Two breaths: in as Bloop grows, out as Bloop shrinks. Then tell your neighbour one thing from today that surprised you, and write it on your sheet.","heading":"Half time · everyone together","seconds":4,"register":"playful","character":"bloop"},"script":"Half time. Two breaths with the whole room, in as Bloop grows and out as Bloop shrinks. Then thirty seconds in pairs on one thing that surprised them, and they write it on their sheet. Nothing is marked and nobody reads theirs out. Then straight into the practise.","minutes":1,"component":"star-breath"},{"type":"interactive","phase":"practise","config":{"options":["Routine ready","Needs a lap","Willpower trap"],"question":"Think of your own after school screen habit, honestly. What is it right now?"},"script":"The whole class check, and honesty is the only rule. Read the question, give ten seconds of silent thinking, then count hands for each answer and tap the bars up. Say it before anyone votes: willpower trap is not a confession, it is the smartest thing in the room to notice, because the whole lesson says routines beat willpower every time. When the bars land, ask one volunteer per bar to say why they voted that way.","caption":"The honest hands up. No wrong answers, only true ones.","minutes":4,"component":"class-tally","extension":true},{"body":"Six situations on your worksheet: after school, bedtime, mealtimes and homework. Read each one like a detective and give your verdict: Routine ready, Needs a lap, or Willpower trap. Every verdict needs a reason, and if your verdict is Needs a lap, write which step is missing: agree, finish or swap. Fifteen minutes. Number six is a big claim from Teo, and your job is to agree or disagree with real reasoning.","type":"tryit","label":"Your turn","phase":"practise","script":"Hand out the worksheet, fifteen minutes on the clock, bookmark strips on tables so the lap is in front of anyone who wants it. Support group works items one to three with you, reading each aloud. Stretch task for early finishers: redesign item three so it earns Routine ready, all three steps included. Circulate and collect one brilliant reason to read out at the end. Listen especially for pupils naming the missing step, that is the skill, not just the verdict.","heading":"Routine detectives, on paper","minutes":15},{"type":"choice","phase":"prove","script":"First of two exit checks, these two are the printed exit quiz, so pupils answer alone, no partner talk. A wrong answer here tells you who to mark as working towards on the register. The second option is the one to watch: any pupil who thinks routine means ban has missed the heart of the module.","minutes":2,"options":[{"text":"Because you decide once, when you are calm, instead of fighting the pull every single time","correct":true,"feedback":"That is the whole lesson in one line. Apps are designed to make the in the moment fight unfair. The routine moves the decision to a moment you can actually win."},{"text":"Because a routine means no screens allowed","correct":false,"feedback":"A routine is not a ban. Screens keep their time, and it is time YOU chose. The routine decides when, not whether."},{"text":"Because willpower only works for adults","correct":false,"feedback":"Honestly, willpower loses for adults too, ask any grown up about their own bedtime scrolling. That is why everyone needs routines, not just children."}],"question":"Exit check one. Why does a routine beat willpower?"},{"type":"choice","phase":"prove","script":"Second exit check, still working alone. This one tests the ownership idea, which is the part that makes tonight's mission real. After answers are in, point at the commitment line on the exit card: tonight's routine, in their own words, the agreed end, the finishing line and the swap all named.","minutes":2,"options":[{"text":"You do, with your family. A routine you built yourself is a routine you actually keep","correct":true,"feedback":"Exactly. Handed down rules get argued with. Your own design gets defended, because it is yours. That is why tonight's mission is yours to build, not to receive."},{"text":"A grown up should hand you the rules, children cannot design routines","correct":false,"feedback":"You just designed one in this lesson. Families help and agree it together, but the design is yours, and that is exactly what makes it stick."},{"text":"Nobody needs to design it, good habits just happen on their own","correct":false,"feedback":"Against apps with no finishing line, nothing just happens except more scrolling. Good routines are built on purpose, by a designer. Tonight that designer is you."}],"question":"Exit check two. Tonight you set one screen routine. Who should design it, and why does that matter?"},{"text":"Agree it. Finish it. Swap it. I designed this routine, so this routine is MINE.","type":"quote","label":"Say it like Bloop","phase":"close","script":"Whole class says it together, twice, louder the second time and everyone hits the MINE. This is the sentence you want repeated in kitchens tonight when the routine gets set.","minutes":1},{"body":" One last thing, and it is the point of the whole lesson. The internet is not the enemy and it is not a treat: it is a place you will spend some of your life, with brilliant things in it and rubbish things in it, often on the same screen. Being good at it means knowing which is which, and choosing on purpose instead of being carried.","type":"recap","phase":"close","points":["Routines beat willpower because you decide once, when you are calm, instead of fighting the pull every time.","The cool down lap: agree the end before you start, finish at a real finishing line, swap to the next thing you lined up.","Screens sleep outside the bedroom, because scrolled minutes are sleep minutes poured away, and they never come back.","A routine you design is a routine you keep. Tonight you set one: after school, bedtime, mealtime or homework."],"script":"Return to the hands up count from the start: who has had one more video turn into loads more? Ask the room what they would do differently tonight and let two or three pupils answer. Then the recap, read by pupils, one point each. Point three gets the cup of water picture one last time.","heading":"What to remember","minutes":2},{"type":"digi","lines":["Your mission, and it is a good one.","Build one screen routine this week: agree, finish, swap.","Stick to it for seven days and see what changes. I think you will like it."],"phase":"close","script":"The handover. Say nothing over it. When the last line lands, ask them to look at you and say the mission once more in your own words. DiGi closes on the next slide.","heading":"Bloop's mission for you","minutes":1,"character":"bloop"},{"type":"interactive","phase":"close","config":{"after":"A full page brings the big check, and the big check earns Bloop's stamp.","button":"Fill the page","prompt":"Today filled a little of the Good habits page. Tap to fill it in.","heading":"The passport","moduleId":"ks2-04-screen-routines","register":"playful","placement":"builder"},"script":"The passport is the book of getting ready for screens and phones, all the way to sixteen, and many of your families keep it at home in the parents app. Every lesson here fills a little of the page, and jobs at home fill it too. One volunteer taps Fill the page; the ring and the area bar move by one, and the class says the word stamp. Say: when the page is full, the big check earns Bloop's stamp. Nobody can fail the passport, it only fills up. The parent note going home says which page today filled and carries the home code that puts it in the child's own book. The count on the wall is this screen's memory of your classes, never a child's record.","minutes":1,"component":"passport-page"},{"type":"digi","lines":["Mission complete, Routine Builders! ⭐","Remember the lap: agree, finish, swap. It works because YOU designed it, and designers stick to their own designs.","Tonight's mission: set ONE routine and run it. After school, bedtime, mealtime or homework. Just one, and make it completely yours.","Bloop will be asking about your badge next lesson. Sleep well tonight, squad, your tomorrow will thank you. See you next time!"],"phase":"close","script":"Let DiGi land the ending, the bubbles appear on their own. Exit quizzes and commitment cards go out as this plays, named copies from the print room. Collect the exit quizzes in, they are your evidence for the register. The commitment card goes home in the bag with the parent note.","heading":"DiGi sends you on your mission","minutes":1}]$gc389$::jsonb,
         parent_note = $gc389${"taught":"Today your child learned why routines beat willpower: apps are designed with no finishing line, so the fix is a better design, not more effort. They learned a three step tool called the cool down lap (agree, finish, swap) and they have committed to setting one screen routine tonight.","headline":"What we taught today, and one thing to try at home","passport":"This lesson has a place on your child's passport page, the journey to sixteen that home and school build together. The class met it today; it goes on the page when your child does its own short check at home, in their app. Each stage ends with a stamp that is earned, never just a birthday reached. Ask your child what their cool down lap is.","try_this":"Tonight, ask your child to design their own cool down lap for bedtime: the finishing line they agree before the screen goes on, and what they swap to. Let them set the terms, and if you can, agree together that screens charge outside the bedroom. A routine they design is a routine they keep.","family_question":"Can you teach me the cool down lap? Explain why a routine beats willpower.","listen_for":"You know apps have no finishing line, so you agree, finish and swap instead of relying on willpower at bedtime.","then_ask":"Where should my phone sleep tonight, and why?","no_login_required":true}$gc389$::jsonb,
         teacher_notes = $gc389${"send":{"eal":"Agree, finish and swap are the three words to pre teach. The routine detective verdicts, ready, lap or trap, work as thumbs once the three words are solid.","sensory":"Autoplay and its pull are described, never demonstrated. Keep it that way for this class: a room full of sound and motion would be teaching the opposite of the lesson.","attention":"The cool down lap is an executive function scaffold by design. Let the pupils who find stopping hardest write the agree step for the class: they are the experts in what does not work.","communication":"The class tally answers by moving a token, not by speaking. Put sentence stems on the board for the routine share: we agree, I finish at, I swap to."},"tool":{"lines":["Agree","Finish","Swap"],"heading":"The cool down lap","strapline":"One lap, then the next good thing. My routine, my design, mine."},"i_can":["I can spot the difference between a routine that works and a trap.","I can run my own cool down lap: agree, finish, swap.","I can explain to someone at home why agreeing the end first makes stopping easier."],"cycles":[{"verb":"Notice","title":"Routines beat willpower","minutes":2,"outcome":"I can spot the difference between a routine that works and a trap."},{"verb":"Practise","title":"The cool down lap","minutes":7,"outcome":"I can run my own cool down lap: agree, finish, swap."},{"verb":"Place","title":"Where screens sleep matters","minutes":10,"outcome":"I can explain why where a screen sleeps changes how well I sleep."},{"verb":"Set","title":"The table and the desk","minutes":9,"outcome":"I can name one place at home where screens are put away, and say why."}],"timing":"69 minutes: starter 11, cycle one 2, cycle two 7, cycle three 10, cycle four 9, practise 16, prove 4, close 6, class interactive 4","keywords":[{"word":"routine","definition":"A plan you decide once and then follow, so you do not have to fight the same battle every day."},{"word":"willpower","definition":"Using effort in the moment to make yourself stop. It runs out, especially when you are tired."},{"word":"wind down","definition":"The calm time before bed when your brain slows down and gets ready for sleep."},{"word":"sleep displacement","definition":"When screen minutes push sleep minutes out of the way. The lost sleep never comes back."}],"equipment":"Nothing beyond the board. The routine planner is in the print room.","exit_quiz":[{"answer":"False","format":"true_false","question":"Stopping is easy if you really try.","teaching_point":"Apps and games are built with no finishing line, so willpower is the wrong tool. This is the misconception to smash."},{"answer":"Agree, finish, swap.","format":"short_answer","question":"What are the three steps of the cool down lap?","teaching_point":"The tool they leave with. Three words, in order."},{"answer":"The end is planned, so it is not a surprise","format":"tick_one","options":["It makes you feel guilty","The end is planned, so it is not a surprise","It turns the screen off faster"],"question":"Why does agreeing the end first work?","teaching_point":"Not willpower, not punishment. The end was decided while calm, so stopping is the plan, not a fight in the moment."},{"answer":"routine","format":"fill_blank","question":"Willpower runs out, especially when you are tired. A ___ does not.","teaching_point":"The headline of the lesson in one line."},{"answer":"Because a screen in the bedroom keeps pulling, and lost sleep never comes back.","format":"short_answer","question":"Why does it matter where a screen sleeps at night?","teaching_point":"Watch for the shallow answer, that it makes a noise. Silent does not fix it, the pull does not need a sound."}],"worksheet":{"title":"Routine detective: ready, lap or trap?","directions":"Read each situation, give your verdict, and write the reason, naming any missing step of the lap.","verdict_options":["Routine ready","Needs a lap","Willpower trap"]},"teacher_tip":"Let pupils design a routine for a character before their own. A routine written for someone else gets written honestly.","starter_quiz":[{"answer":"Any ordered account of their own evening.","format":"short_answer","question":"Describe your evening in order, from getting home to lights out.","teaching_point":"The lesson builds a routine onto their real evening. If they cannot describe it, they cannot redesign it."},{"answer":"Wiggly","format":"tick_one","options":["Wiggly","Hungry for maths","Taller"],"question":"Which of these is a feeling a screen can leave behind?","teaching_point":"Carried forward from ks1-02. The body still notices, and now they can name it."},{"answer":"False","format":"true_false","question":"A rule and a habit are the same thing.","teaching_point":"A rule is enforced from outside. A habit runs on its own. The difference is the whole lesson."},{"answer":"Any genuine habit: brushing teeth, feeding a pet, packing a bag.","format":"short_answer","question":"Name one thing you always do at the same time every day, without being told.","teaching_point":"Proof they already own habits. The lesson is not asking for a new skill, it is pointing at one they have."}],"misconceptions":["Stopping is easy if you really try (apps and games are built with no finishing line, so willpower loses to design, the fix is better design, not more effort)","Putting the phone on silent fixes the bedroom problem (silent stops the pings but not the pull, checking is still one reach away all night, and displaced sleep never comes back)","Routines are punishments adults hand out (this module never bans screens, the child designs the routine, chooses the finishing line and the swap, and ownership is exactly what makes it stick)"],"paper_fallback":"The whole lesson runs from the printed pack. Read the title and mission aloud, teach the keywords from the sheet, run the retrieval question by hands up. Draw the cool down lap on the board as three boxes (agree, finish, swap) and the bedtime spiral as four arrows in a circle. Both scenarios are printed as cards: read them in role, then take verdicts by hands. Discussions run exactly the same with a watch instead of the on screen timer. Worksheet, exit quiz and commitment card are all in the pack.","passport_stage":"builder","commitment_stem":"My commitment: tonight my cool down lap is agree ... before I start, finish when ..., and swap to ...","differentiation":{"stretch":"Early finishers redesign worksheet item three so it earns Routine ready, naming all three steps. Deeper stretch: which of the four moments (after school, bedtime, mealtimes, homework) is hardest to design for, and why does bedtime beat the others for sneaky design like autoplay?","support":"Work worksheet items one to three aloud with the support group, reading each scenario and walking the three verdict options together. Keep the bookmark strip with agree, finish, swap in front of each pupil. Accept the verdict alone for items one and two, then coach the reason."},"prior_knowledge":["A pupil can describe their own evening in order, from getting home to lights out.","A pupil can name a feeling a screen leaves behind, from ks1-02.","A pupil understands that a rule and a habit are different things."],"worksheet_items":[{"n":1,"item":"Maya plays one round after school. Before she starts, she agrees one round and then dinner. She finishes the round, then goes to help set the table.","teaching_point":"All three steps are present: the end agreed before she started, a real finishing line (end of round) and a swap (setting the table). This is what a designed routine looks like on an ordinary day.","expected_verdict":"Routine ready"},{"n":2,"item":"Leo says he will stop scrolling when he feels tired. Autoplay keeps starting the next video. It is now an hour past his bedtime.","teaching_point":"No plan at all, just hoping a feeling will do the work. Autoplay deletes the finishing line, so the feeling never comes. This is the bedtime spiral from the lesson.","expected_verdict":"Willpower trap"},{"n":3,"item":"Priya starts her homework with her tablet face down right next to her. She has no plan for screens tonight, she is just going to concentrate really hard.","teaching_point":"Concentrating really hard is willpower in disguise, and the tablet within reach costs focus even when it is off. A designed version puts the tablet in another room and the fun thing after homework as the swap.","expected_verdict":"Willpower trap"},{"n":4,"item":"It is Saturday and Jess wants a long gaming morning. Before she starts, she agrees with her dad that the last match before lunch is her finishing line, and plans a bike ride with her brother straight after.","teaching_point":"Long screen sessions can be routine ready too. What makes it work is designed time: an agreed end, a finishing line and a swap. Jess designed hers.","expected_verdict":"Routine ready"},{"n":5,"item":"Amara agrees to stop when tea is ready, but tea is always ready mid video, so stopping feels horrible and she keeps sneaking the tablet back to the table.","teaching_point":"The agree step is there but the finish step is missing. Tea arriving mid video is slamming the brakes, which is why it hurts and why the routine keeps breaking. Fix: agree to stop at the end of a video, and add a swap.","expected_verdict":"Needs a lap"},{"n":6,"item":"Teo says: I do not need a routine, I have amazing willpower, I can stop whenever I want. Do you agree with Teo? Explain your reasoning, and use what you know about finishing lines.","teaching_point":"The explain item. Strong answers say the apps are designed with no finishing line by teams of adults, so willpower fights an unfair battle, and that even people with great willpower use routines so they only have to decide once. Disagreeing with Teo kindly, with reasons, is full marks.","expected_verdict":"Willpower trap"}],"essential_question":"Why is it so hard to stop, even when I meant to?","learning_objective":"Pupils can design one screen routine using the cool down lap (agree, finish, swap), explain why routines beat willpower, and commit to setting one routine at home tonight.","key_learning_points":["Willpower runs out, especially when you are tired. A routine does not.","A routine is decided once, in a calm moment, and followed after that.","The cool down lap is agree, finish, swap.","Agreeing the end before you start is what makes stopping possible, because the stop is part of the plan, not a surprise.","Where a screen sleeps decides whether it interrupts your sleep."],"hard_questions":[{"question":"My parents have their phones in bed. Why is it different for me?","answer":"Honestly, it is not, and they would probably tell you the same. The pull works on adults too. Nothing here says only children need a routine. If you want to take the cool down lap home and offer it to the whole house, that is a better version of this lesson than the one I planned."},{"question":"So this is just a way of taking my phone off me?","answer":"No, and if it were I would say so. There is no rule here about how long you get. A routine is you deciding, once, in a calm moment, instead of arguing about it every night when you are tired. You keep the phone. You get the argument back."},{"question":"All my friends are online at night. I will miss everything.","answer":"That is the real cost and I am not going to pretend it is nothing. Being out of the conversation is a genuine loss. What you are weighing it against is sleep you never get back. Only you can do that sum, but do it awake, not at eleven at night."},{"question":"I can stop whenever I want.","answer":"Tonight you probably can. The problem is the night you are tired, and that is the night willpower is lowest and the app is exactly the same strength. A routine is what you set up for that night, by the version of you who is not tired yet."}]}$gc389$::jsonb
   where module_id = 'ks2-04-screen-routines';
end $$;

-- ── KS2: ks2-05-gaming-time-spend ─────────────────────────────────────────
do $$
declare got text;
begin
  select h into got from (
select md5(string_agg(md5(v), '' order by md5(v))) as h, count(*) as n,
       (select jsonb_array_length(slides) from schools.school_lessons where module_id = 'ks2-05-gaming-time-spend') as slides
from (
  select x #>> '{}' as v from schools.school_lessons l, lateral jsonb_path_query(l.slides, '$.**') as x
  where l.module_id = 'ks2-05-gaming-time-spend' and jsonb_typeof(x) = 'string'
  union all
  select x #>> '{}' as v from schools.school_lessons l, lateral jsonb_path_query(l.video_beats, '$.**') as x
  where l.module_id = 'ks2-05-gaming-time-spend' and jsonb_typeof(x) = 'string'
  union all
  select x #>> '{}' as v from schools.school_lessons l, lateral jsonb_path_query(l.assessment, '$.**') as x
  where l.module_id = 'ks2-05-gaming-time-spend' and jsonb_typeof(x) = 'string'
  union all
  select x #>> '{}' as v from schools.school_lessons l, lateral jsonb_path_query(l.parent_note, '$.**') as x
  where l.module_id = 'ks2-05-gaming-time-spend' and jsonb_typeof(x) = 'string'
  union all
  select x #>> '{}' as v from schools.school_lessons l, lateral jsonb_path_query(l.teacher_notes, '$.**') as x
  where l.module_id = 'ks2-05-gaming-time-spend' and jsonb_typeof(x) = 'string'
  union all
  select x #>> '{}' as v from schools.school_lessons l, lateral jsonb_path_query(l.dsl_note, '$.**') as x
  where l.module_id = 'ks2-05-gaming-time-spend' and jsonb_typeof(x) = 'string'
  union all
  select module_id from schools.school_lessons where module_id = 'ks2-05-gaming-time-spend'
  union all
  select title from schools.school_lessons where module_id = 'ks2-05-gaming-time-spend'
  union all
  select key_stage from schools.school_lessons where module_id = 'ks2-05-gaming-time-spend'
  union all
  select year_band from schools.school_lessons where module_id = 'ks2-05-gaming-time-spend'
  union all
  select audience from schools.school_lessons where module_id = 'ks2-05-gaming-time-spend'
  union all
  select evidence_anchor from schools.school_lessons where module_id = 'ks2-05-gaming-time-spend'
  union all
  select single_action_outcome from schools.school_lessons where module_id = 'ks2-05-gaming-time-spend'
  union all
  select character_cast from schools.school_lessons where module_id = 'ks2-05-gaming-time-spend'
  union all
  select scaffold from schools.school_lessons where module_id = 'ks2-05-gaming-time-spend'
  union all
  select unnest(statutory_hooks) from schools.school_lessons where module_id = 'ks2-05-gaming-time-spend'
  union all
  select unnest(ailit_domains) from schools.school_lessons where module_id = 'ks2-05-gaming-time-spend'
) q
  ) z;
  if got = 'aed88edcbf62364f65b1d5b1a8a0d372' then
    return; -- already rewritten
  end if;
  if got is distinct from '0793d8c253cd975fa386e271c1a9b736' then
    raise exception '389: ks2-05-gaming-time-spend does not match its files (hash %), refusing to overwrite a hand edit', got;
  end if;
  update schools.school_lessons
     set slides = $gc389$[{"body":"One hour, one skill: three questions that catch a game reaching for your time and your money.","type":"title","phase":"starter","title":"Gaming: time, intensity and spend","script":"Settle everyone with this slide up. Today the squad goes undercover inside the games you love. Bloop is our lead spotter for this mission. By the end of the hour you will know the tricks that game designers use on millions of players, and you will be able to catch every single one.","eyebrow":"KS2 · Years 3 to 6 · Module 5","minutes":1,"character":"bloop"},{"type":"digi","lines":["Hi! Bloop here.","If a game is free, how is it making money out of you?","Great question. Let us follow the money."],"phase":"starter","script":"Let Bloop land and say the lines. Then read the question on the wall once, in your own voice, and leave it hanging. Do not answer it. The next slide says what today is for, and the class talks before anyone is told.","heading":"Bloop arrives","minutes":1,"character":"bloop"},{"why":"Games are brilliant, and the people who make them are brilliant too, so brilliant that they design chests, countdowns and gem prices to nudge players into paying without noticing. Today you learn their playbook, so every trick has to get past you first.","type":"objective","gains":["Name the tricks games use to keep you playing past your stopping point","Turn gem prices back into real pounds before deciding anything","Spot a raffle dressed up as a treasure chest","Run the three spend spotters on any offer in under a minute"],"phase":"starter","script":"Read the mission out loud, then the why. Quick hands up: who plays games most days? Keep those hands up and say clearly that those hands belong to the experts in this room. This lesson never tells gamers off. It makes gamers sharper. Ask one pupil to read each gain.","minutes":2,"outcome":"I can spot when a game is trying to get me to spend."},{"mode":"pairs","type":"discussion","phase":"starter","prompt":"Tell your partner about a game you like, and one thing in it that costs money.","script":"Sixty seconds. A game you like, and one thing in it you can pay for. I am not going to tell you gaming is bad, because it is not. I want to know what you already notice.","lookFor":"Skins, coins, chests, battle passes. They know the shop is there. Starting from what they already know is what keeps them with you.","minutes":2,"seconds":60},{"type":"keywords","phase":"starter","words":[{"word":"loot box","meaning":"A chest or box you pay for without knowing what is inside. You are buying a chance, not a thing."},{"word":"in game currency","meaning":"Game money like gems or coins that you buy with real money. It hides what things really cost."},{"word":"limited time offer","meaning":"A deal with a countdown clock. The rush is designed to make you buy before you think."},{"word":"odds","meaning":"The chance of winning. 1 in 100 means if 100 people open the chest, about one gets the top prize."}],"script":"Say each word, class repeats it back. Pause on odds, it is the one they will not have met. Ask: if the odds are 1 in 100 and you open two chests, are you sure to win? No. Two chests is about two chances in a hundred, so it is still nearly always nothing. Listen for the idea that enough goes make a win certain, or that a win is owed after a run of losses. You do not need to fix it fully yet, the raffle slide later does that job.","heading":"Spotter words for today","minutes":2},{"type":"choice","phase":"starter","script":"Retrieval from last lesson. Thirty seconds of thinking time, then cold call two pupils for the three words of the lap, agree, finish, swap. Today builds on it, because the reason stopping is hard is not weakness, it is design, and we are about to see the blueprints.","minutes":3,"options":[{"text":"Agree, finish, swap: agree the end before you start, finish the round you are in, then swap to the next thing","correct":true,"feedback":"That is the lap. Agreeing the end first means no surprises, finishing the round means no rage quitting mid match, and swapping straight to something else stops the game pulling you back."},{"text":"Turn everything off instantly the second time is up","correct":false,"feedback":"Instant off mid match feels unfair and starts the battles. The lap works because you finish the round first. That is the deal, and both sides keep it."},{"text":"Ask for ten more minutes, then ten more after that","correct":false,"feedback":"That is not a lap, that is extra time on a loop. The agreed end and the finish are what make stopping feel fair to everyone."}],"question":"Warm up from last lesson. Screen time is nearly up and you are mid game. What is the cool down lap?"},{"body":"Games are one of the best things humans have ever built, full of stories, teamwork and skill. They are also made by teams of clever designers whose job is to keep you playing longer and, in lots of games, to get you spending. Both of those things are true at the same time, and you do not have to pick one. A player who knows the tricks is a stronger player than one who does not.","type":"concept","emoji":"🎮","phase":"teach","script":"This slide sets the tone for the whole hour, so land it carefully. We are not here to say games are bad. Anyone who says games are bad has not played a good one. We are here because the designers know their playbook and most players do not. Ask: is it possible to love something AND know it is trying to keep you there? Take two answers. Yes is the answer, and it is true of games, sweets and telly.","heading":"Brilliant AND designed. Both true.","minutes":2},{"mode":"pairs","type":"discussion","phase":"teach","prompt":"Tell your partner about a time you meant to play for ten minutes and played for a lot longer.","script":"Sixty seconds. A time you meant to play for ten minutes and it was not ten minutes. It happens to me too. Then I am going to show you the tools that did it.","lookFor":"Nobody is in trouble for this and it should not sound like a telling off. You want them noticing the pull, because the next slide names the tools that made it.","minutes":2,"seconds":60,"extension":true},{"type":"diagram","phase":"teach","steps":[{"text":"Matches roll straight into the next one. The game never says done, so you have to say it.","emoji":"🔁","title":"No finish line"},{"text":"Miss a day and you LOSE the streak. That is not a gift, it is a lead pulling you back tomorrow.","emoji":"🔥","title":"Streaks and daily prizes"},{"text":"SO CLOSE! flashes on purpose. Nearly winning makes you press play again faster than winning does.","emoji":"😤","title":"The near miss"},{"text":"Opening the game not because you want to play but because nothing else is happening. The game counts that too.","emoji":"🍿","title":"Boredom snacking"}],"script":"Walk the four tools as they build and give each one ten seconds of real talk. On the near miss, tell the truth: scientists found that nearly winning fires up your brain more than winning, and designers know it. On boredom snacking, ask for a show of hands: who has opened a game, played thirty seconds, closed it, then opened it again? Nearly every hand goes up, including yours if you are honest. That honesty buys you the whole lesson.","caption":"Four tools designers use to stretch your session. Noticing them is a player skill, not a lecture.","heading":"The keep you playing kit","minutes":3},{"mode":"pairs","type":"discussion","phase":"teach","prompt":"Think of a game you love. Which keep you playing trick does it use, and when did you last feel it working on you?","script":"Sixty seconds, partner talk, timer on screen. You are listening for expertise, not confessions. After the chime take three answers and respond like a coach scouting talent: that is a proper spot, that is exactly the near miss, that is the streak lead. Name your own weak spot too, it keeps the room honest.","lookFor":"Pupils naming tricks in real games without shame. Gamers are the experts here. Listen for streak panic and one more match stories, and celebrate honest spotting out loud.","minutes":2,"seconds":60,"extension":true},{"type":"choice","phase":"teach","script":"Whole class vote, hands or letters. When you reveal, connect it back to the cool down lap: this is exactly why the lap has a finish step, because the game will always offer one more. The lap beats the hook when you know the hook is coming.","minutes":2,"options":[{"text":"The game is being generous because you played well","correct":false,"feedback":"The bonus appears exactly when players usually stop. It is not a thank you, it is a hook, and it is timed on purpose."},{"text":"The game noticed a stopping point and is paying you to ignore it","correct":true,"feedback":"Exactly. Stopping points are where players leave, so the game spends pretend coins to keep you. Once you can see the hook, you get to choose on purpose instead of being reeled in."},{"text":"You have to take it or you will fall behind everyone else","correct":false,"feedback":"Double coins tonight changes almost nothing by next week. Falling behind is the feeling the designers rented, not the truth."}],"question":"It is nearly time to stop and the game flashes PLAY ONE MORE MATCH FOR DOUBLE COINS. What is really going on?"},{"body":"Lots of the biggest games cost nothing to download, and that is not the company being kind. Free is the front door. The real shop is inside: gems, coins, chests, passes and limited time offers. The game does not make money when you start playing, it makes money when you keep playing and start paying, so every screen is built to walk you gently towards the shop.","type":"concept","emoji":"💎","phase":"teach","script":"Ask the room to shout out free games they play, and pick a famous one. Then ask the detective question: that game made hundreds of millions of pounds last year, so if it is free, where did the money come from? Let them work it out. Someone always gets there: from the players, inside the game. That is the whole of level two, said by a nine year old.","heading":"Free games that cost","minutes":2},{"type":"stat","claim":"About nine in ten UK children aged 3 to 17 play video games.","phase":"teach","figure":"9 in 10","script":"Let the number land. Nearly everyone in this room, nearly everyone in the country. Then the flip: that is exactly why the spend tricks are worth millions. When almost every child in Britain is a player, even a small trick that works a little bit becomes an enormous pile of money. You are not a customer to these designers, you are the whole market.","source":"Ofcom Children and Parents: Media Use and Attitudes Report 2022","minutes":1},{"type":"choice","phase":"teach","script":"Hands down, think first. Why gems and not pounds. There is a good reason and it is not a kind one.","minutes":2,"options":[{"text":"So your brain stops counting real money.","correct":true,"feedback":"Exactly. Nobody feels three pounds fifty when it says two hundred gems. That is the whole trick, and turning gems back into pounds beats it."},{"text":"Because pounds do not work inside games.","correct":false,"feedback":"They do. The game could show you the price in pounds any time it wanted. It chooses not to."},{"text":"To make it fairer for everybody.","correct":false,"feedback":"It is not about fairness. Ask yourself who it is easier for, the player or the shop."}],"question":"A game sells gems and coins instead of pounds. Why do you think it does that?","extension":true},{"type":"diagram","phase":"teach","steps":[{"text":"You swap pounds for gems and your brain stops counting. 480 gems does not feel like real money. It is.","emoji":"💎","title":"Money becomes gems"},{"text":"The chest costs 480 gems but gems come in packs of 300 or 1,000. You always have spare gems left, pulling you back to the shop.","emoji":"🧮","title":"The bundles never match"},{"text":"ENDS IN 14:32! Rushing you is the point. A rushed brain buys, a calm brain checks.","emoji":"⏳","title":"The countdown clock"}],"script":"Walk the three moves as they build. On the first one, do the magic trick live: would you hand a stranger £4? No? Would you spend 480 gems without blinking? Same money, different costume. On the bundles, ask why no pack ever exactly fits the price, and let them realise it is designed. Nobody accidentally makes every bundle the wrong size.","caption":"Three moves that stop your brain counting real money. Turning gems back into pounds breaks all three.","heading":"How the price hides","minutes":3},{"meta":"In game shop · MEGA CHEST event","text":"⭐ MEGA CHEST ⭐ One LEGENDARY item could be inside... maybe! 480 gems. OFFER ENDS IN 14:32. When the timer hits zero this chest is GONE FOREVER!","type":"scenario","image":"⏳","label":"Evidence item one","phase":"teach","stats":"⭐ Legendary: 1 in 100   🎁 Opened 2.4M times today","avatar":"🎁","handle":"Blocky Legends shop","prompt":"How many tricks can you count on this one screen? Thirty seconds with your partner, then hands up with a number.","script":"Pair talk, thirty seconds, then collect numbers. There are at least four: the countdown rushing you, gems hiding the price, maybe means random like a raffle, and gone forever making it feel once in a lifetime. Point at the small print: 1 in 100. Ask: if the whole class opened one chest each, how many legendaries would we expect? None or maybe none, and the shop keeps everyone's gems either way. Let that sink in.","minutes":2,"platform":"feed","extension":true},{"mode":"groups","type":"discussion","phase":"teach","prompt":"Why do games sell gems instead of just showing prices in pounds? Follow the money: who wins when the price hides?","script":"Groups of three or four, ninety seconds on the timer. This is the follow the money question and KS2 are surprisingly good at it. Collect one answer per group. The line to land at the end: shops that are proud of their prices show them in pounds. When a price wears a costume, ask why.","lookFor":"The company wins twice: hidden prices feel smaller so players spend more, and leftover gems pull players back to the shop. Nobody hides a price to help the buyer.","minutes":2,"seconds":90,"extension":true},{"type":"choice","phase":"teach","script":"Give them a moment with the numbers on screen. If anyone works out that getting the chest actually costs at least £2.99 plus another pack, or £8.99 with 520 gems spare, celebrate loudly, that is exactly the pounds translation the spotters need.","minutes":2,"options":[{"text":"There is no trick, that is just maths","correct":false,"feedback":"The maths IS the trick. Neither pack fits the price, so you either buy big or buy twice, and either way you end up with spare gems pulling you back."},{"text":"The bundle sizes are chosen so you always overbuy and always have leftovers","correct":true,"feedback":"Spotted it. Mismatched bundles are designed, not accidental. Turning gems back into pounds is how you see what you are really being asked to pay."},{"text":"The trick is that gems are worthless","correct":false,"feedback":"Gems do buy real things in the game, that is what makes the trick work. The problem is not that gems are fake, it is that they stop your brain counting pounds."}],"question":"A chest costs 480 gems. Gems come in packs: 300 gems for £2.99 or 1,000 gems for £8.99. What is the trick?","extension":true},{"body":"A loot box works like a raffle. You pay real money for a ticket, and you MIGHT win the amazing prize, but most tickets win something small you did not really want. The chance of the top prize is usually tiny, and the game knows the exact odds even when you do not. Paying money for a random chance is how gambling machines work, which is why some countries have banned loot boxes for children. You are not silly for wanting the prize. The whole machine is built by experts to make everyone want the prize.","type":"concept","emoji":"🎟️","phase":"teach","script":"This is the honesty slide, so go slow and keep it kind. Ask who has been to a school fair raffle. Everyone pays, one person wins the hamper, the raffle keeps all the money. A loot box is that, but the raffle can follow you home and ask again every day. Say the gambling word plainly and calmly: adults have machines that work exactly like this, they are called gambling machines, and there are strict laws about them because they are so hard to stop feeding. Loot boxes work the same way, and that is a fact, not a telling off.","heading":"Random like a raffle","minutes":2},{"type":"diagram","phase":"teach","steps":[{"text":"Countdown clocks, ends tonight, last chance. Real deals survive thinking time.","emoji":"🏃","title":"Spotter one: is it rushing me?"},{"text":"Gems, coins, tokens, mismatched bundles. Turn it back into pounds before you decide anything.","emoji":"🕵️","title":"Spotter two: is it hiding the price?"},{"text":"Paying for a CHANCE is not shopping, it is a raffle ticket. Know the odds or walk away.","emoji":"🎟️","title":"Spotter three: is it random like a raffle?"}],"script":"This is the tool of the module, so build it with energy. Get the class chanting the three questions back: is it rushing me, is it hiding the price, is it random like a raffle. Then point at the three verdicts and make this crystal clear: fine to enjoy is a real verdict. Spending in games is not banned and not shameful. A clear price, no rush, no raffle, and a grown up who said yes is a perfectly good purchase. The spotters are not a wall, they are a torch.","caption":"Three questions before any money moves. And real money always goes through a grown up.","heading":"The spend spotters","minutes":3,"verdicts":["Fine to enjoy","Ask first","Walk away"]},{"meta":"Squad chat · 7:48pm","text":"everyone in the squad has the dragon bundle now, it is only 999 gems and the event ends TONIGHT. just use your mum's card, she will not even notice. you are literally the only one without it 😬","type":"scenario","image":"🐉","label":"Evidence item two","phase":"teach","avatar":"🕹️","handle":"Finn 🎮","prompt":"Run all three spend spotters, then spot the fourth pressure that is not even from the game. What would you say back to Finn?","script":"Read Finn's message aloud with real pressure in your voice. Then take it apart together: ends tonight is the rush, 999 gems is the hidden price, and the fourth pressure is a friend, which is the strongest one of all. Be clear that Finn is not a villain, Finn got caught by the same countdown and now it is spreading through him. Then the two hard lines, said kindly: a card that is not yours is never yours to use, not even a little bit, and a real friend hearing I need to ask first says fair enough. Script a reply together, something like: might get it, asking first, catch you in the lobby.","minutes":2,"platform":"message","extension":true},{"type":"interactive","phase":"practise","config":{"prompt":"Two breaths: in as Bloop grows, out as Bloop shrinks. Then tell your neighbour one thing from today that surprised you, and write it on your sheet.","heading":"Half time · everyone together","seconds":4,"register":"playful","character":"bloop"},"script":"Half time. Two breaths with the whole room, in as Bloop grows and out as Bloop shrinks. Then thirty seconds in pairs on one thing that surprised them, and they write it on their sheet. Nothing is marked and nobody reads theirs out. Then straight into the practise.","minutes":1,"component":"star-breath"},{"type":"interactive","phase":"practise","config":{"label":"The spend spotters · tap the class verdict","posts":[{"why":"Spending is not the enemy, tricks are. Clear price, no rush, no raffle, permission given: all three spotters pass, and that verdict matters as much as the others.","text":"The game shop shows a sword skin for £1.99. The full price is on the label in pounds, there is no timer, and you have pocket money saved and permission to spend it.","answer":0,"avatar":"🎮","handle":"Case 1"},{"why":"All three spotters fire at once: the countdown rushes you, gems hide the price, and a 1 in 100 chance is a raffle ticket. When all three fire, walking away is the strong move.","text":"MEGA CHEST: 480 gems for a CHANCE at a legendary item. The legendary odds are 1 in 100 in tiny writing. OFFER ENDS IN 14:32!","answer":2,"avatar":"🎮","handle":"Case 2"},{"why":"The bundle mismatch is the hiding trick: £8.99 for 40 gems of need leaves 960 spare gems pulling you back to the shop. Translating it into pounds out loud is what asking first is for.","text":"You run out of gems mid battle. A popup offers 1,000 gems for £8.99, but the thing you want only needs 40 more gems.","answer":1,"avatar":"🎮","handle":"Case 3"},{"why":"The rush plus a friend's pressure plus a card that is not yours. A card that is not yours is never yours to use, and a real friend accepts I need to ask first.","text":"A friend messages the squad chat: everyone has the dragon bundle, the event ends tonight, just borrow your mum's card, she will not notice.","answer":2,"avatar":"🎮","handle":"Case 4"},{"why":"The free first taste plus the near miss is the raffle machine warming you up. SO CLOSE is designed, the wheel was never nearly anything.","text":"SPIN THE WHEEL! Your first spin is free and you win 50 gems. The next spin costs 99p and the top prize flashes SO CLOSE, TRY AGAIN!","answer":2,"avatar":"🎮","handle":"Case 5"},{"why":"Leo is half right, companies do need money, and honest clear prices are the fair way to earn it. But nearly won is the near miss doing its job, and free means the shop inside IS the product. Credit answers that respect Leo while catching both tricks.","text":"Leo says: it is fine, the game is free so they have to make money somehow, and anyway I nearly won a legendary last time. Do you agree with Leo? Explain your verdict.","answer":2,"avatar":"🎮","handle":"Case 6"}],"doneBody":"Same six cases on the sheet now. This time every verdict needs its reason written down.","verdicts":["Fine to enjoy","Ask first","Walk away"],"doneEmoji":"⭐","doneTitle":"All six sorted"},"script":"The spend spotters go to work together before the sheet. Read each card, hands up for the verdict, tap the class answer, read the reason. Draw out the middle verdict every time it fires: ask first is not a lesser answer, it is the spotter working.","caption":"Sort the six cases together before the sheet.","minutes":4,"component":"verdict-sort","extension":true},{"body":"Your teacher has six offers on the worksheet: shop screens, popups and chat messages. Run the three spend spotters on each one and give your verdict: fine to enjoy, ask first, or walk away. You have fifteen minutes. Every verdict needs a reason, a verdict without a reason does not count.","type":"tryit","label":"Your turn","phase":"practise","script":"Hand out the worksheet. Fifteen minutes, bookmark strips on the tables so the three questions are in front of anyone who wants them. Support group works items one to three with you as a guided group. Stretch question for early finishers is on the plan: design the sneakiest FAIR shop screen you can. Circulate and collect one brilliant reason to read out at the end.","heading":"Spend spotter patrol, on paper","minutes":15},{"type":"choice","phase":"prove","script":"First of two exit checks, done individually, this is your evidence of who got it. Watch for pupils choosing the third answer, the all games are scams position feels streetwise but it is the same lazy thinking as buying everything. The spotters exist because the honest answer is it depends, and now they can work out what it depends on.","minutes":2,"options":[{"text":"The timer means it is a great deal, so buy fast before it goes","correct":false,"feedback":"The timer IS the trick. It exists to rush you past thinking. A deal that is only good for ten minutes is a deal that is scared of your brain."},{"text":"It is rushing me, so I pause and ask first. If it is really worth it, it is still worth it after asking","correct":true,"feedback":"That is the spotters working. Rushing detected, so the calm brain takes over: pause, translate the price, ask a grown up. Most timed offers come back around anyway."},{"text":"Never buy anything in a game, it is all a scam","correct":false,"feedback":"Spending you chose calmly, at a clear price, with permission, on a thing you can actually see is fine. The spotters are a check, not a ban."}],"question":"Exit check one. A skin bundle you really want shows a timer: 09:59 left. You have birthday money saved. What do the spend spotters say?"},{"type":"choice","phase":"prove","script":"Second exit check. This one proves they understood the raffle idea, which is the deepest learning of the day. A pupil who can explain the difference between buying a thing and buying a chance has got everything this module wanted to give them.","minutes":2,"options":[{"text":"No. In a shop you pay and get the thing you chose. With the chest you pay for a chance, like a raffle ticket","correct":true,"feedback":"Exactly right. Shopping is money for a thing. A loot box is money for a maybe, and the game sets the odds. That difference is the whole reason spotter three exists."},{"text":"Yes, paying is paying, it makes no difference","correct":false,"feedback":"It makes all the difference. A shop must hand you what you chose. A chest can hand almost everyone the small prize and keep the money, and the odds say it usually will."},{"text":"No, because chest prizes are never real","correct":false,"feedback":"The prizes are real, that is what makes it clever. The problem is not fake prizes, it is that you are paying for a random chance with tiny odds of the one you want."}],"question":"Exit check two. A chest costs 400 gems and gives a random prize. Your friend says paying for it is just like buying a toy in a shop. Is he right?"},{"text":"Is it rushing me? Is it hiding the price? Is it random like a raffle? Spend spotters, spotted it!","type":"quote","label":"Say it like Bloop","phase":"close","script":"Whole class says it together, twice, louder the second time, with Bloop's playful energy. Point at each question on your fingers as they chant. This is the sentence you want shouted at a loot box screen in a living room this weekend.","minutes":1},{"type":"recap","phase":"close","points":["Games are brilliant AND designed to keep you playing and paying. Both things are true at once.","The keep you playing kit: no finish line, streaks, near misses and boredom snacking. Noticing them is a player skill.","The spend spotters: is it rushing me, is it hiding the price, is it random like a raffle?","Real money always goes through a grown up, and anything truly worth buying is still worth buying after you ask."],"script":"Four pupils read one point each. Then return to the start of the lesson: you walked in as players, you are walking out as players who have read the designer's playbook. Nothing about the game changed in the last hour. Everything about the player did.","heading":"What to remember","minutes":2},{"type":"digi","lines":["Your mission.","Next time a game rushes you, hides the price, or spins a raffle, say it out loud: spend spotter!","Spotting it is the whole trick. You have got this."],"phase":"close","script":"The handover. Say nothing over it. When the last line lands, ask them to look at you and say the mission once more in your own words. DiGi closes on the next slide.","heading":"Bloop's mission for you","minutes":1,"character":"bloop"},{"type":"interactive","phase":"close","config":{"after":"A full page brings the big check, and the big check earns Bloop's stamp.","button":"Fill the page","prompt":"Today filled a little of the Good habits page. Tap to fill it in.","heading":"The passport","moduleId":"ks2-05-gaming-time-spend","register":"playful","placement":"builder"},"script":"The passport is the book of getting ready for screens and phones, all the way to sixteen, and many of your families keep it at home in the parents app. Every lesson here fills a little of the page, and jobs at home fill it too. One volunteer taps Fill the page; the ring and the area bar move by one, and the class says the word stamp. Say: when the page is full, the big check earns Bloop's stamp. Nobody can fail the passport, it only fills up. The parent note going home says which page today filled and carries the home code that puts it in the child's own book. The count on the wall is this screen's memory of your classes, never a child's record.","minutes":1,"component":"passport-page"},{"type":"digi","lines":["Mission complete, squad! ⭐","Three spend spotters, faster than a loading screen: is it rushing me, is it hiding the price, is it random like a raffle?","Your mission this week: catch ONE trick in a game you love and name it out loud. Countdown clock, gem costume, mystery chest. Spotted it!","The games companies have teams of expert designers. From today, every one of them has to get past YOU. See you next lesson!"],"phase":"close","script":"Let DiGi land the ending, the bubbles appear on their own. Exit quizzes go out as this plays, named copies from the print room, and pupils write their commitment line on the exit card before they leave. Collect them in, they are your evidence for the register.","heading":"DiGi wraps the mission","minutes":1}]$gc389$::jsonb
   where module_id = 'ks2-05-gaming-time-spend';
end $$;

-- ── Proof ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
do $$
declare got text;
begin
  if (select slides from public.lessons where id = '3cc159b1-f3cd-46b9-9eb1-fc29c7899e40') is distinct from $gc389$[{"body":"A sofa lesson about endings. We practise saying goodbye to the screen so the goodbye stops being scary.","type":"title","phase":"starter","title":"When the screen goes to sleep","script":"Recall first: what is our stop and tell rule? Stop, and tell a grown up. Today is a different kind of goodbye: how we say bye bye to the screen when it is time.","eyebrow":"Foundation · Ages 4 to 7 · Healthy balance","minutes":1,"character":"football"},{"why":"Endings feel big when they are a surprise. Our plan makes the ending small: a stopping place, a wave, a next thing.","type":"objective","gains":["I know our stopping place, like the end of the episode","I can wave goodbye to the screen","I know the next thing comes straight after"],"phase":"starter","minutes":1,"outcome":"I can say goodbye to the screen calmly when we reach our stopping place."},{"type":"keywords","phase":"starter","words":[{"word":"the stopping place","meaning":"Where the screen stops, picked before it goes on, like the end of the episode."},{"word":"the next thing","meaning":"What we run to after the screen: a snack, a story, a job."}],"script":"Say each word and let your child say it back. The stopping place and the next thing are the two halves of every calm ending.","heading":"Our special words","minutes":1},{"body":"Big feelings come when the screen stops in the middle of something. So we pick the stopping place before the screen goes on: one episode, then snack. When the episode ends, the screen is done.","type":"concept","emoji":"🏁","phase":"teach","script":"Say it the same way every time, before you press play: one episode, then it is snack. Then the show ends it, not you, and there is no countdown to argue with. When the end comes, tell them what comes next, not how long is left.","heading":"The stopping place","minutes":2},{"body":"When time is up, we wave and say bye bye screen, see you tomorrow. Then we go straight to the next thing: a snack, a story, a job helping your grown up. Endings are easier when something nice is waiting.","type":"concept","emoji":"👋","phase":"teach","script":"The wave sounds silly and works like magic: it gives the ending a shape. Have the next thing genuinely ready before the wave happens. A child running toward something is not fighting what they left.","heading":"Wave goodbye, then run to the next thing","minutes":2},{"type":"choice","phase":"practise","minutes":2,"options":[{"text":"Wave bye bye to the screen and go to the next thing","correct":true,"feedback":"Yes. The wave finishes it, and the next thing is waiting for you."},{"text":"Hold the screen tight and look away from your grown up","correct":false,"feedback":"Holding tight makes the goodbye bigger and slower. The wave makes it small and quick."}],"question":"The episode has finished, and that was our stopping place. What do we do?"},{"type":"choice","phase":"prove","minutes":2,"options":[{"text":"So I know where the end is before I start","correct":true,"feedback":"Exactly. You knew the end before the screen went on, so it is not a surprise."},{"text":"So I can ask for one more when we get there","correct":false,"feedback":"The stopping place is the end, not a place to ask for more. When we get there, the next thing starts."}],"question":"Why do we pick the stopping place before the screen goes on?"},{"type":"choice","phase":"prove","minutes":2,"options":[{"text":"Because episodes are always short","correct":false,"feedback":"Some are short, but that is not the magic. The magic is that the show has finished, so nothing gets cut off."},{"text":"Because the show has finished, so nothing is cut off","correct":true,"feedback":"That is it. Big feelings come from being stopped in the middle. At the end of the episode, the show is already done."},{"text":"They do not, endings feel horrible anyway","correct":false,"feedback":"Endings feel much easier when the show has already finished. Nothing gets cut off at our stopping place."}],"question":"Why do endings feel easier at the end of an episode?"},{"type":"choice","phase":"prove","minutes":2,"options":[{"text":"Grabbing the tablet back for one more go, so the cross feeling stops","correct":false,"feedback":"One more go makes the ending come round again, and the cross feeling with it. Wave bye bye and run to the next thing."},{"text":"Waving bye bye and running to the next thing","correct":true,"feedback":"The wave finishes it, the next thing catches you. Cross feelings shrink fast when your hands are busy."},{"text":"Shouting until the feeling goes away by itself","correct":false,"feedback":"Shouting feeds the cross feeling. A snack, a story or a job helping shrinks it much faster."}],"question":"The screen is done and your body still feels cross. What helps most?"},{"type":"recap","phase":"close","points":["We pick the stopping place before we start.","Wave bye bye screen, see you tomorrow.","Run straight to the next thing together."],"heading":"The lesson in three ticks","minutes":1},{"body":"Before bed, let your child choose what comes straight after screen time tomorrow, and have it ready. A snack, a story, a job with you. Their choice, waiting for them.","type":"tryit","phase":"close","script":"A next thing they chose themselves pulls twice as hard as one you announce at switch off time.","heading":"Pick tomorrow's next thing tonight","minutes":1},{"type":"digi","lines":["A stopping place, a wave, a next thing.","Endings are easier when nothing is a surprise.","Bye bye screen, see you tomorrow."],"phase":"close","heading":"DiGi says","minutes":1}]$gc389$::jsonb
     or (select the_idea from public.lessons where id = '3cc159b1-f3cd-46b9-9eb1-fc29c7899e40') is distinct from $gc389$Little children do not end screen time, the ritual ends it for them. A stopping place picked before it starts, a goodbye to the screen and a next thing to run to turn the hardest moment of the day into a routine.$gc389$
     or (select try_this from public.lessons where id = '3cc159b1-f3cd-46b9-9eb1-fc29c7899e40') is distinct from $gc389$Before the screen goes on, pick the stopping place together: one episode, then snack. When it ends, let your child wave goodbye and have the next thing ready. Tell them what comes next, not how long is left.$gc389$
